AB  - <h4>Background</h4>Locally advanced rectal cancer (LARC) is a highly heterogeneous disease, which leads to variable responses among patients undergoing neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (nCRT).<h4>Objective</h4>This study aims to investigate the predictive value of visualization features of intratumoral heterogeneity (ITH) derived from habitat imaging based on diffusion-weighted MRI (DWI-MRI) for tumor downstaging (TDS) after nCRT in LARC patients.<h4>Methods</h4>Two hundred and two primary LARC patients confirmed by pathology (training dataset, n = 101; test dataset, n = 101) were enrolled in our retrospective study. All patients underwent preoperative nCRT treatment and were subsequently categorized into TDS and non-TDS groups based on their therapeutic response. Radiomics feature analysis was conducted on the DWI-MRI images after preoperative nCRT treatment to extract the overall tumor radiomics characteristics and ITH-related ecological diversity features of TDS after nCRT. Clinicopathological (ClinicoPath) characteristics were also screened to construct prediction models and corresponding subregion-specific models. The predictive performance was evaluated by receiver operator characteristic (ROC) curve analysis, and then assessed with respect to its calibration, discrimination and clinical usefulness.<h4>Results</h4>Among the 202 patients, 82 (40.59 %) were classified into the TDS group, while 120 (59.41 %) were assigned to the non-TDS group. Univariate logistic regression identified modified tumor regression grade (mTRG) and N stage as independent clinicopathological predictors of TDS following nCRT. The ITH-Radiomics model exhibited superior predictive performance compared to the Radiomics and ClinicoPath models, achieving area under the curve (AUC) values of 0.901 (95 % CI: 0.697-0.928) in the training set and 0.794 (95 % CI: 0.592-0.867) in the test set. Among subregion models, subregion 2 demonstrated the highest predictive efficacy than subregions 1 and 3, with AUCs of 0.820 (95 % CI: 0.633-0.888) and 0.645 (95 % CI: 0.468-0.718) for the training and test sets, respectively. The combined model achieved the highest predictive efficacy in predicting TDS in LARC patients after nCRT, with AUCs of 0.941 (95 % CI: 0.869-0.978) and 0.928 (95 % CI: 0.749-0.966) for the training and test sets, respectively, decision curve analysis confirmed its clinical usefulness.<h4>Conclusion</h4>This study demonstrated that the extracted ITH visualization features on DWI-MRI based on intratumoral ecological diversity can effectively improve the prediction of TDS in LARC patients undergoing preoperative nCRT and potentially guide treatments to select patients for a "watch-and-wait" approach.
