AB  - <title>Abstract</title> <p>Background Accurate preoperative prediction of lymph node metastasis (LNM) is important for risk stratification and individualized treatment planning in rectal cancer. This study aimed to develop and internally validate a contrast-enhanced CT-based clinical-radiomics model for preoperative prediction of LNM in patients with rectal cancer. Methods This retrospective study included 268 patients with rectal cancer who underwent radical resection and preoperative contrast-enhanced CT. Patients were randomly assigned to a training cohort (n = 187) and a validation cohort (n = 81). A total of 2,264 radiomics features were extracted from venous-phase contrast-enhanced CT images. Variance thresholding, K-best selection, and LASSO regression were used for feature selection. Clinical predictors associated with LNM were identified using univariate and multivariate logistic regression. Clinical, radiomics, and clinical-radiomics models were constructed and evaluated using receiver operating characteristic curves, calibration curves, the Hosmer-Lemeshow test, and decision curve analysis. Results Distance from the anal verge, albumin level, maximum tumor diameter, and radiomics score were identified as important predictors of LNM. The clinical-radiomics model achieved an area under the curve of 0.939 (95% CI: 0.900-0.977) in the training cohort and 0.833 (95% CI: 0.805–0.961) in the validation cohort. The combined model showed better overall performance than either the clinical model or radiomics model alone. Calibration curves showed good agreement between predicted and observed risks, and decision curve analysis indicated greater clinical net benefit across a range of threshold probabilities. Conclusions The contrast-enhanced CT-based clinical-radiomics model showed favorable discrimination, calibration, and clinical utility for preoperative prediction of LNM in rectal cancer. This model may provide a noninvasive tool to assist individualized risk stratification and treatment decision-making.</p>
