AB  - <title>Abstract</title> <p>Background Reliable prediction of disease-free survival (DFS) may help identify patients with locally advanced rectal cancer (LARC) who remain at high risk of disease recurrence or death after neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (NCRT) and radical surgery, thereby supporting more individualized postoperative risk stratification. This study aimed to develop and internally validate a clinicoradiomics model integrating pretreatment MRI-derived radiomics features with clinicopathological factors to predict postoperative DFS. Methods A total of 292 patients with LARC who underwent NCRT followed by radical surgery were retrospectively enrolled. Radiomics features were extracted from pretreatment T2-weighted imaging (T2WI) and diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI). Patients were randomly divided into training cohort (n = 233) and testing cohort (n = 59). Feature selection was performed using variance filtering, correlation analysis, univariable and multivariable Cox regression. Independent clinicopathological prognostic factors were identified. Clinical, radiomics, and combined models were constructed and evaluated using time-dependent area under the curve (AUC), time-dependent concordance index (C-index), Kaplan–Meier analysis, calibration curves, and decision curve analysis (DCA). Results During a median follow-up of 56.95 months, 58 patients experienced DFS events, including 52 distant metastases, 3 local recurrences, and 3 deaths without documented recurrence. The combined model incorporating radiomics score, age, pathological tumor deposits, and extramural vascular invasion showed better predictive performance compared with the clinical and radiomics models alone. In the training cohort, the combined model achieved time-dependent AUCs of 0.815 and 0.822 and time-dependent C-indexes of 0.789 and 0.783 at 36 and 60 months, respectively. In the testing cohort, the corresponding AUCs were 0.812 and 0.814, and the C-indexes were 0.781 and 0.783. High-risk patients classified by the combined model had significantly poorer DFS compared with low-risk patients (training cohort HR = 5.67, 95% CI: 2.73–11.77, P < 0.001; testing cohort HR = 9.90, 95% CI: 1.82–53.79, P < 0.01). Conclusions Integration of pretreatment MRI-derived radiomics features with clinicopathological factors may improve postoperative DFS prediction in LARC patients. The combined model may provide a preliminary tool for individualized postoperative DFS risk stratification, pending prospective multicenter validation.</p>
