AB  - <title>Abstract</title> <p>Background: Accurate prediction of early recurrence and survival in patients with colorectal cancer (CRC) is essential for personalized treatment. Conventional TNM staging has limited accuracy. We aimed to develop and split-sample validate machine learning (ML) models for early recurrence and mortality using routine electronic health record data. <h4>Methods:</h4> In a retrospective multicenter cohort of 5,136 stage I–III CRC patients after radical surgery, we compared three approaches: TNM stage only, multivariate Cox regression, and ML (gradient boosting survival analysis for survival, CatBoost classifier for recurrence risk). The dataset was split 80:20 into training (n=4,108) and split-sample validation (n=1,028) datasets via stratified sampling by event status and time intervals. Missing values were imputed or handled natively. The synthetic minority oversampling technique for Nominal and Continuous (SMOTENC) was applied to address class imbalance (13.5% recurrence) in the training set. No calibration methods were applied. <h4>Results:</h4> TNM stage alone showed poor discrimination (AUC 0.48). Cox regression performed poorly for survival outcomes (split-sample validation AUC 0.46 [95% CI: 0.42–0.50] for overall survival and 0.47 [95% CI: 0.43–0.52] for recurrence-free survival) but showed good performance for recurrence risk (AUC 0.78 [95% CI: 0.74–0.81]). The gradient boosting survival analysis model achieved split-sample validation C indices of 0.677 for overall survival and 0.693 for recurrence-free survival. CatBoost achieved a split-sample validation AUC of 0.728 [95% CI: 0.698–0.758] for recurrence. <h4>Conclusion:</h4> Unlike Cox regression, which fails to predict survival outcomes (AUC ~0.46–0.47), the gradient boosting survival analysis model provides modest but stable prognostic information for overall survival (C-index 0.677) and recurrence-free survival (C-index 0.693) after split-sample validation. Prospective validation is warranted. The code is available upon request.</p>
